    "username": "a16z",
    "nickname": "a16z",
    "text": "http://MakerMaker.AI co-founders Dhaval Adjodah and Owen He with a16z's Martin Casado on recursive self-improvement, and how to tell when it's real:\n\nA lot of RSI pitches are really just people using AI to build better AI. Tech has always worked that way. You needed computers to build chips. Steam engines got better once you could get coal faster.\n\nDhaval and Owen's bar is higher. The model has to improve itself: its weights, its harness, even the prompt that tells it what to work on next.\n\nAnd the gains have to hold up when you count what they cost. They track everyone's experiment spend on a \"bleeder board,\" and one engineer, the Viceroy of Variance, has the job of breaking their results.\n\nOver the past few months, they've started seeing early signs of their system getting better and cheaper at the same time.\n\n0:55 Why most RSI pitches miss\n2:25 Optimizer vs. optimizee\n5:10 The RSI equation: performance over cost\n6:50 Why a closed loop does not mean takeoff\n8:40 Humans are agents inside the system too\n9:25 The denominator trap: progress divided by capital raised\n11:40 Training GPT-2 for $100\n13:40 Most RSI papers fail when replicated\n16:40 Why you have to co-optimize\n17:40 Why inference is the first problem\n20:15 The agent rewrites its own prompt\n22:20 \"It's not a belief. It's happening.\"\n25:00 Why they haven't published\n27:50 Intelligence is a vector and Amdahl's law\n29:45 The garden hose theory of RSI\n\nYouTube: https://youtu.be/3Q1mdn5ZGvM\n\n@makermaker_ai @_dval_ @martin_casado",
